# ASSERT(3X)

## NAME

assert, static_assert - program verification

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <assert.h>

void assert(scalar expression);

static_assert(constant-expression, string-literal);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Assert* is a macro that indicates *expression* is expected to be true at this point in the
program. It writes a diagnostic to the standard *error* file and calls abort(3) when
*expression* is false (0). Compiling with the b6cc(1) option **-DNDEBUG** effectively deletes
*assert* from the program.

**Note:** v7's page said it "causes an exit(2) with a diagnostic comment on the standard
output". Two corrections there: **the diagnostic goes to fd 2,** and the macro calls *abort*
rather than exit(1), which C11 sec 7.2.1.1 requires and which is worth having - v7's exit(1)
was indistinguishable from an ordinary failure.

**Note:** *static_assert* is C11 and had no v7 ancestor. It is a macro spelling of the
**_Static_assert** keyword, which the front end implements, so it costs nothing.

## DIAGNOSTICS

`Assertion failed: expression, file f, line n`

**Note:** v7 printed the file and line only. *F* is the source file and *n* the source line
number of the *assert* statement.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/assert.c, include/assert.h

## SEE ALSO

abort(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

### <assert.h> is deliberately unguarded, and it is the only such header here

C11 sec 7.2 says NDEBUG is re-examined at *every* inclusion, so a source may include the
header, define NDEBUG, and include it again to turn assertions off for the rest of the
translation unit. An include guard would silently defeat that, so the file opens with **#undef
assert** instead.

### assert is an expression

v7's macro expanded to a *brace block,* so **if (p) assert(q); else ...** was a syntax error
and **assert(q), f()** would not parse at all. C11 sec 7.2.1.1 requires a **void** expression,
and this one is a conditional.

v7's macro also called *fprintf* and *exit* without declaring either, which no longer compiles:
the front end has no implicit declarations.

### The handler writes with write(), not through stdio

There is no stdio dependency here at all, and that is deliberate: an assertion failure is
exactly the moment not to depend on a buffered stream, which is the thing most likely to be
broken already. Perror(3) writes the same way and for the same reason.

Nothing is #included by <assert.h> either - **_Noreturn** is a keyword and the handler is
declared in the header - so a source that asserts need not drag in stdio or stdlib to do it.

### _assert is kept

v7's alias, for the v7 sources that still spell it that way.
